speechtext:
Mr. BORDEN moved that the House do to-morrow go into a Committee of the A hole to consider the following proposed resolution:
Resolved, that it is expedient, (a) to extend the boundaries of the province of Manitoba northward to the sixtieth parallel of latitude and northeastward to the shores of Hudson Bay, upon such terms and conditions 2? ma/ „"e agreed to by the legislative assembly of Manitoba and by parliament;
(b)	to authorize the Governor in Council, inasmuch as the province was not in debt at the time of its establishment, to pay to the said province an annual sum of $381,584 19 being the equivalent of interest at five per cent on $7,631,683.85, the difference between a principal sum of $8,107,500 and $475,816.15 heretofore advanced by the government to the province for provincial purposes; such annual sum to be paid as and from the first day of July, 1908, but subject, however, as to payments due upon the coming into force of any Act founded upon these resolutions, to the deduction of certain sums received by the said province by way of interest on capital allowance in lieu of debt;
(c)	to authorize the Governor in Council to to the said province, in lieu of ownership
of public lands, aij annual sum based upon the population of the said province as ascertained by each quinquennial census thereof, such annual sum to commence with $562,500 (less certain deductions in respect of what are known as swamp lands and university lands) and not to exceed the annual sum of $1,125,000 (less equivalent deductions); the same to be paid as from the first day of July, 1908, but subject, however, as to payments due at the time of the passing of any Act founded upon these resolutions, to a deduction of the aggregate of certain sums received by the pro-

It does not seem to me that the amendment of my hon. friend (Mr. Kyte) is at all objectionable from the standpoint taken by the Minister of Finance (Mr. 112}
White, Leeds). The hon. minister said that the effect might be to interfere with the orderly conduct of the affairs of the commission. But as I read it, it would not compel them to hear evidence at any particular time, but they might appoint such time as would be convenient for them to hear witnesses who might desire to give their testimony. It seems to me the Minister of Finance forgets one very important object of the commission. He deals with the subject as if their whole duty would: be to carry on investigations into specific applications for tariff changes. But, as he knows, the most important work this commission would have to do, would be to consider tariff matters, the cost of raw material, the cost o'! goods imported into Canada, the cost of manufacture in Canada, and so on, with a view to making general tariff revisions from time to time. Surely that is a matter in respect of which anybody in this country who wishes to be heard ought to have a right to go before the commission and give his testimony with facts in support of the views he may have upon tariff matters. Surely, this board ought not to be allowed to say whom they will -call and what opinions they will give and make a report based upon those opinions only. This commission is to have very general powers, and its report must necessarily have great effect upon public opinion and upon parliament. If ever there was a commission in regard to Which the public ought to have the right, by rep_re-senitatfives_ or otherwise, to express their views, this is that commission. The hon. minister refers to the railway commission and says that people cannot go before that commission of their own motion. I have not the law before me, -but I happen to know that the Board of Railway Commissioners have regarded it as a part of their duty to allow every man to come before them who desires to be heard, whether that particular person may have been summoned or not. They do not require a person to be teubpcenaed, but allow people to go voluntarily and state tbei-r grievances and tbeygive what relief thev find possible.
This commission is of enormous importance as regards- tariff matters, it is a matter of great public interest; and it seems to me its duties can only be satisfactorily discharged if people should have an opportunity of coming voluntarily before it, and giving it such facts as thev are able to supply.
